<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaullism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaullism is the French political current that follows the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, stressing national independence, a strong state and a powerful head of state. It grew from the Free French and the Resistance and shaped the constitution of the Fifth Republic in 1958. Gaullist parties, from the Rally of the French People to its successors, have been a main force of the French right.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaullism.jpg" alt="Gaullism" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yousuf Karsh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Generaal_De_Gaulle,_buste_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Gaullism took shape around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, who as head of the Free French Forces became the symbol of resistance to the Nazi occupation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> government. The historian Serge Berstein divides its history into phases. In the first, from 1940 to 1945, it meant those who rejected the armistice with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and followed de Gaulle. From 1946 to 1958 it was an opposition to the Fourth Republic, whose unstable parliamentary government Gaullists wanted replaced by a president with pre-eminent constitutional powers. In 1947 de Gaulle founded the Rally of the French People, whose membership reached one million within six months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Berstein holds that Gaullism is neither a doctrine nor a political ideology, but a pragmatic exercise of power. Its fundamental principle is what de Gaulle called a certain idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which required a strong state able to ensure that the nation's interests came first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">De Gaulle</a> disliked the regime of the parties, which he held responsible for the military disaster of 1940, and wanted a strong executive embodying national unity. He admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> as makers of consensus. In foreign policy Gaullists sought independence from allies, which led to France's own nuclear force, the withdrawal of French forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s command structure and the removal of US bases.</p><p>After 1945 Gaullists at first defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>. De Gaulle later accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>, and he ended the Algerian crisis of 1954 to 1962 by granting independence, which he judged to be in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s interest. Social Gaullism also proposed an association of capital and labour in place of class struggle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">De Gaulle</a> returned to power in 1958 and served as president of the new Fifth Republic from 1959 until his resignation in 1969. His followers organised the Union for the New Republic, which became the largest force after the November 1958 elections. His successor Georges Pompidou consolidated Gaullism between 1969 and 1974. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-of-lorraine">Cross of Lorraine</a> became the symbol of later parties, among them the Rally for the Republic, founded by Jacques Chirac in 1976. Scholars distinguish traditional, social and neo-Gaullism, and the strong presidency was kept by all de Gaulle's successors, including the socialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaullism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government included <b>Gaullists</b>, communists, socialists and Christian democrats from the Resistance.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-french-republic">Provisional Government of the French Republic</a></li></ul></section>
